Change(genesis='66602cd0dec37d58d03eec9518ca805a7ac38ea912d2b0c366a0b67b4b9f50b6', deps=(), event=Decided(decision='closedworldmarkup', at=datetime(2026, 10, 2, 0, 0, 0), actor='assistant', title="Removal is checkable and a blocklist is not: the agent's typst is checked by round trip and by the closed world", body="Removal is checkable; a blocklist is not. So the write verbs don't blocklist bad typst: they build an AST, render it, re-parse it, and refuse to write if the result differs (`suzatary_core::decoration::render_verified`, rendering with `decoration::value` and reading back with `decoration::read` — one typst value language, read by typst's own parser; deliberately NOT through `Render`, because `Render::html` takes an `Escaped` and only confirmed content yields one). The same principle keeps markup safe (2026-10-02): the agent's `body`, `detail` and `detail_append` are TYPST MARKUP, and what it can produce is a fact about the closed world's library, not a scan of its text. It CAN write typst's own elements (emphasis, lists, headings, math, raw, `#link` to any scheme, a `#let` of its own). It CANNOT name `html` or `plugin` (removed, not refused), so it writes no element or attribute typst's own markup would not (no `data-intent`, no `<button>`, no `style`); it cannot read a file, fetch a package or the clock; a `javascript:`/`data:` href or a script compiles and is refused by the tripwire at the door; a loop is killed and refused. Its text is still provisional: marked `(unconfirmed)` where it stands and served as SOURCE until `suzatary todo confirm`.", rationale="The agent's markup compiles in the closed world since 2026-10-02.", supersedes=()))